<h1>Breaking Two Toy Ciphers</h1>
<p class="muted">Statistics 2026. A small public-key scheme on secp256k1 with a restricted private key, the Caesar cipher, and attacks on both. Everything runs in your browser.</p>
<div class="box"><b>In short.</b> Key spaces: Caesar has 26 keys, the toy ECC scheme has 2<sup>n</sup> keys (here 2<sup>8</sup> to 2<sup>32</sup>), real secp256k1 has about 2<sup>256</sup>. Caesar is broken by letter statistics. The toy ECC scheme is broken by an exact key search. A real ECC key is broken by neither.</div>

<h2>1. A toy scheme on secp256k1</h2>
<p>The curve is secp256k1: <code>y² = x³ + 7</code> over the prime field of size <code>p = 2²⁵⁶ − 2³² − 977</code>, with a fixed base point <b>G</b>. The private key is a number <b>d</b> and the public key is the point <b>Q = d·G</b>. Here <b>d</b> is drawn from a small range, below 2<sup>n</sup>, so that attacks can be run in a few seconds.</p>
<p>The scheme itself is a small hybrid scheme in the style of ElGamal and ECIES. The curve is used to agree on a secret point, and a hash of that point is used as a keystream.</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Key generation (receiver).</b> Pick d with n bits, publish Q = d·G, keep d secret.</li>
<li><b>Encryption (anyone, using Q).</b> Pick a fresh one-time number r. Compute C₁ = r·G and the shared point S = r·Q. Turn S into a keystream K = SHA-256(x(S) ‖ 0), SHA-256(x(S) ‖ 1), … and set C₂ = M ⊕ K. The ciphertext is (C₁, C₂).</li>
<li><b>Decryption (receiver, using d).</b> Compute S = d·C₁. This is the same point, because d·(r·G) = r·(d·G). Rebuild K and compute M = C₂ ⊕ K.</li>
</ol>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Public?</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><b>d</b> private key</td><td>No</td><td>Lets the receiver rebuild S from C₁. Whoever knows d can decrypt everything sent to Q.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Q = d·G</b> public key</td><td>Yes</td><td>Lets anyone create a shared point with the receiver without a secret meeting. Safe to publish only because getting d back from Q is the discrete logarithm problem.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>r</b> one-time key</td><td>No, thrown away</td><td>Makes every encryption different. Using the same r twice breaks the scheme (section 8).</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>C₁ = r·G</b></td><td>Yes, sent</td><td>Tells the receiver where to start, without revealing r.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>S</b> shared point</td><td>No</td><td>Same value on both sides: r·Q = d·C₁. Its x-coordinate feeds the keystream.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

