<h2>3. Similarities and differences</h2>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Caesar cipher</th><th>Toy ECC scheme</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Type</td><td>Symmetric: encryption and decryption use the same key.</td><td>Public-key: encrypt with Q, decrypt with d.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Encryption</td><td>Add k to each letter mod 26.</td><td>Compute r·G and r·Q, hash x(S) into a keystream, XOR with the message.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decryption</td><td>Subtract k from each letter mod 26.</td><td>Compute d·C₁ to get S, rebuild the keystream, XOR again.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Randomness</td><td>None. The same message and key always give the same ciphertext.</td><td>A fresh r each time, so the same message gives a different ciphertext every time.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Key space</td><td>26 keys (25 that change anything), about 4.7 bits.</td><td>Here 2<sup>n</sup> values of d. In the real curve d has about 2<sup>256</sup> values.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost of encrypting</td><td>One addition per letter.</td><td>Two scalar multiplications and some hashing.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can a guess be checked?</td><td>Only by reading the result: a wrong key gives gibberish.</td><td>Yes, exactly: d is right if and only if d·G = Q.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hides letter statistics?</td><td>No. Each letter always maps to the same letter, so frequencies are only relabelled.</td><td>Yes. The keystream is pseudo-random, so the same letter becomes different bytes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>What the attacker sees</td><td>Letters with the language structure intact.</td><td>C₁ (a point) and bytes that look random.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Integrity</td><td>None. Anyone can change letters.</td><td>None either: flipping a bit of C₂ flips the same bit of the message (section 8).</td></tr>
<tr><td>What protects it</td><td>Only the secrecy of a tiny key.</td><td>The elliptic-curve discrete logarithm problem: getting d back from Q = d·G.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Both schemes turn a message into a ciphertext with a key, and both are undone by running the right operation backwards. They differ in who holds which key, in how big the key space is, and in how much of the plaintext structure survives. The experiment below makes the last point visible: it encrypts the corpus text from section 4 with both schemes and measures the statistics of the output.</p>
