<h2>4. Statistical attack on the Caesar cipher</h2>
<p>Natural language is not random. In English, E, T, A, O are common and Q, X, Z are rare. A Caesar shift only relabels the letters, so the ciphertext has the same frequency profile as English, rotated by k places. The attack compares the two.</p>
<ol>
<li>From a corpus of English text, count how often each letter occurs. This gives the expected frequencies f<sub>0</sub> … f<sub>25</sub>.</li>
<li>For each candidate key s from 0 to 25, undo the shift and count the letters of the result: O<sub>j</sub>.</li>
<li>Compute the chi-squared distance χ²(s) = Σ (O<sub>j</sub> − n·f<sub>j</sub>)² / (n·f<sub>j</sub>), where n is the number of letters.</li>
<li>Choose the s with the smallest χ². It is the key whose decryption looks most like English.</li>
</ol>
<label style="width:100%">Corpus (any English text, editable)</label>
<textarea id="corpus" rows="7">The old harbour town lies at the mouth of a slow brown river, where the land flattens out and the water spreads into a wide estuary. For most of its history it was a place of small boats, salt, and fish. Families lived close together along narrow streets that ran down to the quay, and almost everyone worked in some way for the sea. In the early morning the fishermen pushed their boats into the water while the women mended nets on the stones, and by noon the market was full of the day's catch. Traders came from the hills with wool and grain, and they left with barrels of salted herring that would feed people far from the coast.

The town grew rich slowly. A stone wall was built around the harbour after a raid from the sea, and a bell tower was added so that the watchman could warn the people when strange ships were seen. Later, when the wall was no longer needed, the merchants used its stones to build houses with tall windows and heavy doors. Some of these houses still stand today, and visitors often stop to look at the carved signs that once told people what each family sold. A baker had a loaf above his door, a rope maker had a coil, and a doctor had a small blue bottle.

In the nineteenth century the railway arrived, and everything changed. The fish could now travel in a single night to the big cities, and the price of a good catch doubled. New factories were opened near the river, and workers from the villages came to live in rows of plain brick houses. Not everyone was pleased with the noise and the smoke, but few could deny that the town had become more important than it had ever been. The old harbour was deepened so that larger ships could enter, and a lighthouse was built on the point to guide them safely through the fog.

Today the factories are quiet and the railway carries mostly visitors. The people who live in the town say that the best time of year is the autumn, when the light is low and golden and the air smells of smoke and apples. On Sunday afternoons they walk along the river path, past the reeds where the herons stand, and talk about the weather, the price of bread, and what their children have been doing at school. There is a small museum near the quay with a model of the harbour as it was three hundred years ago, and the woman who looks after it will gladly explain how each building was used. If you ask her about the great storm, she will tell you that it was the worst anyone remembers, and that it began on a calm evening with nothing but a few thin clouds in the sky.</textarea>
