<h2>5. How much text does the attack need?</h2>
<p>The test uses a second text that the model has never seen (below). For each length, random pieces of that text are encrypted with a random key from 1 to 25, and each method has to find the key. All methods get the same ciphertexts:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>χ², own corpus.</b> The attack from section 4, with frequencies learned from the corpus above.</li>
<li><b>χ², textbook table.</b> The same test with a standard table of English letter frequencies (E 12.7%, T 9.1%, A 8.2%, …) instead of the small corpus.</li>
<li><b>Most frequent letter is E.</b> The quick manual trick: assume the commonest ciphertext letter is E.</li>
<li><b>Bigram scoring.</b> Candidates are scored by the log-probability of their letter pairs, learned from the corpus. It uses the order of letters, not only their counts.</li>
<li><b>Exhaustive search with a dictionary.</b> All 26 keys are tried, and a candidate scores the total length of the common English words it contains. This one also uses the spaces, so it knows where words start and end, which the others do not.</li>
</ul>
<details><summary>Test text (held out, editable)</summary>
<textarea id="testtext" rows="7">When I first learned to cook, I made every mistake that a beginner can make. I burned the rice, forgot the salt, and once left a pan of onions on the stove while I answered the telephone. My grandmother, who had watched me from the doorway, said nothing at first. Then she took the spoon from my hand and told me that cooking is mostly a matter of paying attention. A good cook, she said, is not someone who follows a recipe perfectly, but someone who knows when the food is ready by its smell, its colour, and the sound it makes in the pan.

So we started again from the beginning. She showed me how to cut an onion without crying, how to tell when oil is hot enough, and why a soup should never be allowed to boil too hard. Every afternoon we cooked something simple together, and every evening we sat at the kitchen table and ate it slowly. I remember the first time she tasted a dish of mine and nodded. It was only a plate of beans with garlic and bread, but I felt as proud as if I had won a prize.

Years later I travelled to the mountains by train, carrying a small bag and a notebook full of her advice. The journey took most of a day. We passed long valleys where sheep stood on the green slopes, and little stations with names that I could not pronounce. A man across from me shared his cheese and told me about the village where he was born, a place so high in the hills that the snow stayed until the end of spring. When I said that I liked to cook, he laughed and said that in his village everyone did, because the winters were long and there was nothing else to do but eat well and tell stories.

That evening I stayed in a house at the edge of the village. The owner, an old woman with strong hands, invited me into her kitchen and let me watch her make bread. She worked the dough without measuring anything, and when I asked how she knew how much flour to add, she smiled and said that her hands knew. I thought of my grandmother at once. It seemed to me that all good cooks, in every country, learn the same quiet lesson, and that the lesson cannot be written in any book, however carefully the pages are filled.</textarea></details>
