<h3>Effectiveness and limits</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Length is what matters.</b> Success drops quickly below about 20 letters, where the histogram is too noisy and several keys give a similar χ². With a few dozen letters the right key is already far ahead of all others.</li>
<li><b>Short texts are ambiguous in principle.</b> A 4-letter ciphertext such as <span class="mono">ALEC</span> has several readings that are all plausible English. Statistics cannot decide, and nothing else can either.</li>
<li><b>The corpus must match the text.</b> The model assumes ordinary English. A text without E, a list of names, code, or another language shifts the profile, and the attack can pick a wrong key with great confidence.</li>
<li><b>The "E" shortcut is unreliable.</b> It needs a text where E really is the commonest letter. It fails often below a few hundred letters, and it also fails on the Italian sample, where A, E and I compete. The χ² attack, which uses the whole profile, still finds the key for that sample with the English model, but a language with a very different profile would break it too.</li>
<li><b>Bigram scoring wins on short texts.</b> It uses the order of letters and not only their counts, so it needs fewer letters than the frequency attack. The price is a bigger model: 676 pair counts learned from a corpus of only about 2,000 letters are noisy, and a larger corpus would help.</li>
<li><b>Only letters count.</b> Spaces, digits and punctuation pass through the cipher unchanged and carry no information about the key, although the dictionary method uses the word breaks.</li>
</ul>

<h2>6. Attacking the restricted ECC key</h2>
<p>The attacker knows G, the public key Q, the ciphertext (C₁, C₂), and that d is smaller than 2<sup>n</sup>. The attacker does not know d. Two searches are shown, and both find d from Q alone, without looking at the message.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Exhaustive search.</b> Start from G and keep adding G: G, 2G, 3G, … until the point equals Q. The step counter j at that moment is d. It takes at most 2<sup>n</sup> steps and about half of that on average. The code uses Jacobian coordinates, so each step is a few multiplications mod p and no inversion.</li>
<li><b>Baby-step giant-step.</b> Write d = i·m + j with m = ⌈√2<sup>n</sup>⌉. Store the baby steps jG for j = 1 … m in a table. Then walk Q, Q − mG, Q − 2mG, … until a point is in the table. Each hit gives d = i·m ± j. It takes about 2·√2<sup>n</sup> steps, so n = 32 needs about 130,000 steps instead of 4 billion. The price is a table of 2<sup>n/2</sup> points. The table depends only on G and the key range, so it can be reused for every public key in that range.</li>
</ul>
<p class="muted">Once d is known, decryption is the normal procedure: S = d·C₁, rebuild the keystream, XOR. The target below is whatever was produced in section 1.</p>
