<h3>How this differs from the attack on Caesar</h3>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Caesar (section 4)</th><th>Restricted ECC (this section)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>What is searched</td><td>26 keys.</td><td>2<sup>n</sup> values of d.</td></tr>
<tr><td>How a guess is judged</td><td>By how much the decryption looks like English. This is statistical and can be wrong.</td><td>By an exact test: d·G = Q. No uncertainty, no language involved.</td></tr>
<tr><td>What it needs</td><td>Enough ciphertext, and a model of the plaintext language.</td><td>Only the public key. The message can be one byte or a gigabyte.</td></tr>
<tr><td>More ciphertext helps?</td><td>Yes, that is the whole idea (section 5).</td><td>No. Q already fixes d uniquely.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Why it works</td><td>The cipher keeps the structure of the plaintext.</td><td>The key space is too small to hide anything.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>In short, the Caesar attack is statistics, because the key space is trivial and the weakness is the cipher's leak of letter frequencies. The ECC attack is plain search, because the cipher leaks nothing statistical. The ciphertext in section 3 looks uniform. Only the size of the key space makes the scheme breakable.</p>

<h2>7. Why this does not work on a real curve</h2>
<p>With the real private-key space, d has about 256 bits. The bars are on a log scale (each full bar is about 10<sup>72</sup> seconds). The table uses the speed measured in this browser just now to estimate the cost of the same two attacks. It is a rough extrapolation, not a measurement.</p>
<p class="muted" id="rates"></p>
<div class="scroll"><table id="extra"><thead><tr><th>Key bits n</th><th>Exhaustive search (average)</th><th>Baby-step giant-step time</th><th>Table memory</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
<ul>
<li>For n = 256, exhaustive search needs about 2<sup>255</sup> ≈ 5.8×10<sup>76</sup> steps on average. At this browser's speed that is many orders of magnitude longer than the age of the universe (1.4×10<sup>10</sup> years).</li>
<li>Baby-step giant-step and Pollard's rho cut the work to roughly 2<sup>128</sup> steps. That is still about 3×10<sup>38</sup> steps. Baby-step giant-step also needs a table of that size, which no computer can store. Pollard's rho avoids the table but not the time.</li>
<li>No known classical algorithm solves the discrete logarithm on a well-chosen curve much faster than this square-root bound. This is the hardness that the public key relies on. A large quantum computer running Shor's algorithm would break it, but none exists today. This is why post-quantum standards are being developed.</li>
<li>Doubling the key size does not double the work. Each extra bit doubles the exhaustive search and multiplies the square-root attack by √2.</li>
</ul>

<h2>8. Other weaknesses of the toy scheme</h2>
<p>Some weaknesses come from the small key space. Others come from the way the toy scheme is designed, and they would remain with a 256-bit key.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Weakness</th><th>Comes from</th><th>Shown below?</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>d found by searching all values or by baby-step giant-step</td><td>Restricted key space</td><td>Section 6</td></tr>
<tr><td>r also drawn from a small range: r found from C₁ in the same way, then S = r·Q gives the keystream without d</td><td>Restricted key space (of r)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reusing r for two messages: the keystreams are equal, so C₂ ⊕ C₂′ = M ⊕ M′ and known text in one message reveals the other</td><td>Design (and bad randomness)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ciphertext can be changed without the key: flipping a bit of C₂ flips the same bit of M</td><td>Design: no authentication</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Message length is visible, there is no padding</td><td>Design</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>A decryptor that does not check that C₁ is on the curve can be fed invalid points. On secp256k1, whose group has prime order, a simple on-curve check is enough</td><td>Design, implementation</td><td>The code checks it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Anyone can encrypt to Q, so the receiver cannot tell who sent a message</td><td>Design: no sender authentication</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>The receiver key d is fixed, so if d leaks later, every old ciphertext can be opened (no forward secrecy)</td><td>Design</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>The keystream is a bare hash of x(S), with no context or key-derivation step</td><td>Design</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>One baby-step table serves every victim with the same key range</td><td>Restricted key space</td><td>Section 6</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
