<h2>9. Conclusions</h2>
<ul>
<li>Caesar and the toy ECC scheme both hide a message behind a key and are undone by the matching operation. Caesar is symmetric, deterministic and leaks letter frequencies. The ECC scheme is public-key, uses a fresh r each time and produces output that looks uniform.</li>
<li>Caesar falls to statistics with a few dozen letters (about 20 to 40 in the test above). The restricted ECC scheme falls to a search that needs no knowledge of the language and no statistics.</li>
<li>The security of ECC comes from the huge key space together with the lack of a shortcut for the discrete logarithm. Shrinking the key space removes it, and a careless design can break it even with a large one.</li>
</ul>

